-- title:
--   Descent of Uₚ and a diamond to the Raynaud quotient
-- statement:
--   **Setting.** Fix a prime $p$ and a level $M \neq 0$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^{2} \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$; the hypothesis `hHp` requires that every unit $u$ of $\mathbb{Z}/M$ whose image under `ZMod.unitsMap` for $(M/p) \mid M$ is trivial lies in $H$, and $M/p$ is nonzero. Fix a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $Pl$ (this is `Pl.LiesOverPrime p`), whose residue field is of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed. The hypothesis `hj` says that the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jqModC ℚ`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) of $j$ lies in the field [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101); it is the input needed to form the two-chart integral models `toBase p Γ hj` over the ring $R_p =$ `ratLocalizedAt p`. Further data: $\mathfrak{X}$, a Deligne–Rapoport-type integral model datum `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` at $p$; $\Lambda$, level data `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl` (a section $\sigma_A$ of the base, a scheme $X$ with structure morphism $f$, a relative group law $L$ on it, and identifications of the generic and special points with $J_H(M/p)$-ype and $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ groups); and $O$, an object `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, i.e. a smooth separated commutative relative group scheme $g \colon G \to \mathrm{Spec}\,R_p$ whose generic points are identified with $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \overline{F}_H)$, carrying Hecke endomorphisms `O.hecke`, a toric rank `O.toricRank`, and the associated subgroups `O.finPts m` (generated by the $m$-torsion classes whose section extends over the place $\sigma_A$) and `O.toricPts m` (generated by the toric points).
--
--   **Representability group (`hrep`, `hrepΛ`).** Two nonemptiness hypotheses assert that $(G, g)$ together with the unit section represents, in the sense of `RepresentsRelSubPic` (existence of a Poincaré rigidified line bundle satisfying the condition, its universal property, and triviality along the zero section), the subfunctor `algEquivZeroCut` of fibrewise algebraically trivial rigidified line bundles on the integral model at level $\Gamma_M$ with the cusp section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$; and that $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ with its unit section does the same at level $\Gamma_N$, with the section obtained from $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$.
--
--   **The place ring (`hRA`, `hRloc`, `hres`).** $R_h$ is a henselian local domain with an injective algebra map to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ whose image lies in $Pl$; the maximal ideal of $R_h$ is exactly the set of $x$ with $Pl$-valuation of $\mathrm{alg}(x)$ less than $1$; and $R_h$ is a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra for which $\mathrm{alg}(x) = 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}/p$ holds under the same valuation condition, so that $\mathbb{Z}/p$ is the residue field of $R_h$. A set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ and a unit $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ index the Hecke generators used below.
--
--   **The $p$-divisible group and its comparison with $J_H$ (group $(\Delta, e)$).** $\mathcal{G}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup Rh p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199): levels $\mathcal{G}_v$ that are finite free cocommutative Hopf $R_h$-algebras of rank $p^{vh}$, with surjective transition bialgebra maps whose kernels are the $p^{v}$-torsion ideals. Given are an additive map $\Delta$ from the direct limit $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $J_H(M)$ and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ of Tate modules (sequences $(x_n)$ with $p^{n}x_n = 0$ and $px_{n+1} = x_n$), subject to: `hΔinj` ($\Delta$ injective); `hΔlev` (for all $v$, the image under $\Delta$ of the level-$v$ points is exactly `O.finPts (p ^ v)`); `hΔgal` ($\Delta$ is equivariant for $\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ acting through $R_h$-automorphisms agreeing with the given one); `hΔhecke` (for every set $S$ and generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) — a $T_\ell$, a $U_q$ or a diamond — there is a transition-compatible family of bialgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of the levels inducing `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$); `he` ($e$ is computed coordinatewise by $\Delta$); `heinj`; `herange` (the range of $e$ consists of the Tate elements whose $n$-th coordinate lies in `O.finPts (p ^ n)`); `hegal` (compatibility of $e$ with the Galois actions on the two Tate modules); `hsat` (the range of $e$ is saturated: $p y$ in the range forces $y$ in the range); `hcoker` (the cokernel of $e$ is $\mathbb{Z}_p$-free of rank `O.toricRank`); and `htor` (every element of `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point).
--
--   **The quotient $\mathcal{B}$ (group $\psi$).** $\mathcal{B}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup Rh p hB`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) with $h =$ `O.toricRank` $+ h_{\mathcal{B}}$ (`hhB`) and $h_{\mathcal{B}} = 2h'$ for some $h'$ (`hhB2`), and $\psi_v \colon \mathcal{B}_v \to \mathcal{G}_v$ is a family of $R_h$-bialgebra maps (so, on spectra, a family of homomorphisms from $\mathcal{G}$ to $\mathcal{B}$) with: `hψt` (compatibility with the transition maps); `hψker` (a level-$v$ point $x$ of $\mathcal{G}$ has trivial image along $\psi_v$ precisely when $\Delta(x) \in$ `O.toricPts (p ^ v)`); `hψsurj` (every level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{B}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ comes from one of $\mathcal{G}$); `hψred` (if the $\psi_v$-image of $x$ agrees with the counit to valuation $< 1$ on all of $\mathcal{B}_v$, then $x$ does so on all of $\mathcal{G}_v$); and `hperiod` (for $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, a $p^{v}$-torsion class $z$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ and a level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma z - z$, the $\psi_v$-image of $y$ agrees with the counit to valuation $< 1$).
--
--   **The integral realisation (group $(\rho_h, \iota)$).** A ring map $\rho_h \colon R_p \to R_h$ compatible with the two algebra maps into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρh`), and morphisms $\iota_v \colon \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{G}_v \to G$ satisfying: `hιbase` (each $\iota_v$ lies over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho_h$); `hιcl` (the resulting morphism into the pullback of $g$ along $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho_h$ is a closed immersion); `hιp` ($\iota_v$ followed by multiplication by $p^{v}$ is the unit section over the base); `hιpts` (for every level-$v$ point $x$, the section of $O$ attached to $\Delta(x)$ is $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$); `hιmul` ($\iota_v$ turns the convolution product of points with values in any commutative $R_h$-algebra into the relative group law `O.L.mul`); `hιt` (compatibility with the transitions); `hιhecke` (for every $S$ and generator $g$ there is a transition-compatible family $\varphi_v$ of bialgebra endomorphisms which intertwines $\iota$ with `O.hecke S g` and induces `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$); and `hιfin` (for each $v$, the canonical comparison morphism $j_v$ from the $p^{v}$-kernel, formed as the pullback of multiplication by $p^{v}$ against the unit section, into the base change of that kernel along $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho_h$ is both an open and a closed immersion, and every point of that base change lying over the closed point of $R_h$ is in the image of $j_v$).
--
--   **The operators to be descended.** $u_v$ is a family of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels $\mathcal{G}_v$, transition-compatible (`hut`), intertwining $\iota$ with the Hecke endomorphism `O.hecke S (CohCarrier.Gen.U p _ hpM)` (`huι`); $D_{p,v}$ is a family of $R_h$-bialgebra automorphisms of the levels, transition-compatible (`hDpt`), intertwining $\iota$ with `O.hecke S (CohCarrier.Gen.dia d)` (`hDpι`). Both preserve the toric points through $\Delta$: `hutor` and `hDptor` state that if $\Delta(x) \in$ `O.toricPts (p ^ v)`, then the same holds for the point obtained by composing $x$ with $u_v$, respectively with $D_{p,v}$. Finally `hjB` requires that for each $v$ the base change $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \psi_v$ (the bialgebra tensor map of the identity of $\mathbb{Z}/p$ with $\psi_v$) be injective.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist a family $u_{B,v}$ of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels $\mathcal{B}_v$ and a family $D_{B,v}$ of $R_h$-bialgebra automorphisms of the levels $\mathcal{B}_v$ such that:
--
--   1. for every $v$, $u_v \circ \psi_v = \psi_v \circ u_{B,v}$;
--
--   2. for every $v$, $D_{p,v} \circ \psi_v = \psi_v \circ D_{B,v}$;
--
--   3. for every $v$, $(\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{transition}\;v) \circ u_{B,v+1} = u_{B,v} \circ (\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{transition}\;v)$;
--
--   4. for every $v$, $(\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{transition}\;v) \circ D_{B,v+1} = D_{B,v} \circ (\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{transition}\;v)$.
--
--   Thus the $U_p$-operator and the diamond operator indexed by $d$, realised on $\mathcal{G}$, descend to endomorphism and automorphism families on the quotient $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$, compatibly with the levels.
--
--   This is the descent step in the analysis of the $p$-divisible group attached to the Néron object of $J_H(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing $M$: the Hecke operator $U_p$ and a diamond operator, given on the full $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ and preserving the toric points, are transported to the quotient $\mathcal{B}$ obtained by killing the toric part. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_descent_torusQuotient_raynaudQuotient_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_injective`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_descent_torusQuotient_raynaudQuotient_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_injective), which combines this descent with the torus-quotient side of the same witness.
